    summary: 'A core is the unit that executes one instruction stream. The front end fetches and decodes instructions into micro-ops, the rename stage removes false register dependencies, and an out-of-order back end issues micro-ops to execution ports as soon as their inputs are ready, retiring them in program order from a reorder buffer. Almost every host-side step of an LLM request (HTTP parsing, tokenization, Python dispatch, scheduler bookkeeping, kernel launches) is a single-thread latency problem on one such core.',
    purpose: 'Extract instruction-level parallelism from sequential code so that a single thread runs fast despite cache misses and branches.',
    mechanics: 'The front end fetches 16-32 bytes per cycle from L1I, predicts the next fetch address, and decodes 4-6 x86 instructions per cycle into micro-ops (commonly served from a decoded-uop cache on hits). Rename maps architectural registers onto a much larger physical register file (hundreds of entries) so that write-after-read and write-after-write hazards disappear. Micro-ops enter a reorder buffer of the order of 300-500 entries (example: recent Intel and AMD server cores) and a scheduler picks ready ones each cycle for 8-12 execution ports: integer ALUs, branch units, vector/FP units, load ports and store ports. Loads and stores go through a load/store queue that enforces memory ordering and forwards store data to later loads. Retirement is in order; a mispredicted branch or an exception flushes everything younger than it. Achieved IPC is typically 1-3 on real serving code, far below the machine width, because of cache misses, branch mispredictions and dependency chains. Single-thread latency therefore depends on frequency, IPC and the miss profile, not on core count; a scheduler loop or a tokenizer does not get faster by adding cores. The `perf stat` counters `instructions`, `cycles`, `branch-misses` and `cache-misses` give IPC and the top-level reasons for stalls, and top-down analysis (`perf stat --topdown`) attributes slots to front-end, bad speculation, back-end or retiring.',
