    summary: 'Coherence guarantees that all cores observe a single value per memory location even though each has private caches. A write to a line first obtains exclusive ownership, which invalidates every other copy; the next reader on another core then misses and fetches the line from the writer. This is invisible to correct code but sets the cost of shared counters, locks, atomics and any hot structure that several threads touch.',
    purpose: 'Make private caches safe under multithreading, so that locks and shared data behave as if there were one memory.',
    mechanics: 'Each line in each cache is in a state such as Modified, Exclusive, Shared or Invalid (MESI; Intel adds Forward, AMD adds Owned). A store to a Shared or Invalid line issues a read-for-ownership that invalidates other copies and upgrades the local one to Modified; a later load on another core sees a miss and receives the line via a cache-to-cache transfer, commonly 30-70 ns within a socket and 100-200 ns across sockets (example ranges). Protocol traffic is tracked by snoop filters or directories at the L3 or memory controller so that not every request broadcasts. False sharing is the case where two threads write unrelated variables that happen to sit in the same 64-byte line, so the line ping-pongs between cores although no data is logically shared; padding or `alignas(64)` fixes it. Atomic read-modify-write operations (locked instructions, `fetch_add`) on one hot line serialize at the owning cache and scale poorly beyond a few cores; a request counter or a global queue head updated per token by many threads becomes a coherence bottleneck. Reader-heavy sharing is cheap: many cores can hold a Shared copy until someone writes. Cross-socket coherence rides the CPU interconnect (UPI, Infinity Fabric) and is slower and lower bandwidth than on-die traffic, which is one reason to keep a thread pool inside one NUMA node. `perf c2c` reports the lines with the most HITM (hit-in-modified) events, which are the false-sharing and contention hot spots.',
