    summary: 'A core may execute and buffer memory operations out of program order, so another core can observe them in a different order unless the architecture or explicit fences forbid it. x86 implements total store order: loads and stores stay ordered except that a store may become visible after a later load. ARM and other weakly ordered architectures allow far more reordering. Any lock-free structure that moves work between an API server thread and an engine thread, or between a CPU thread and a GPU via mapped memory, is correct only if it uses the right atomics and barriers.',
    purpose: 'Define the contract under which threads on different cores can communicate through shared memory without a lock.',
    mechanics: 'Stores go into a per-core store buffer and drain to L1 later, so under TSO a load can be satisfied before an earlier store is globally visible (store-load reordering); a `mfence` or a locked instruction drains the buffer. Under ARM’s weak model, loads can also pass loads and stores can pass stores, so `dmb` barriers or load-acquire / store-release instructions (`ldar`/`stlr`) are needed to publish data. The C++ and Rust memory models abstract this: a producer writes the payload, then stores a flag with release semantics; the consumer loads the flag with acquire semantics and is then guaranteed to see the payload. On x86, acquire and release loads and stores compile to plain moves, sequential consistency stores cost a locked instruction or `mfence`; on ARM they compile to real barriers, so code that only worked because of x86’s strong order breaks when moved to ARM servers or Apple silicon. Atomic read-modify-writes (`lock xadd`, `cmpxchg`, ARM `ldxr/stxr` or LSE atomics) are the building blocks of counters, queues and locks and cost tens of cycles uncontended, far more when contended. The compiler is also allowed to reorder non-atomic accesses, so a volatile or a plain variable is never a substitute for an atomic. A single-producer single-consumer ring between the HTTP server thread and the scheduler loop, or a doorbell written by the CPU and polled by a GPU kernel through pinned memory, uses exactly this pattern: payload first, release-store the index, acquire-load on the other side. CUDA has its own scoped memory model (`__threadfence`, `cuda::atomic_ref` with system scope) for the GPU end of such a channel.',
