    mechanics: 'Each NUMA node exposes a set of CPUs and a range of physical memory; `lscpu` and `numactl -H` list them, and `nvidia-smi topo -m` shows which node each GPU and NIC hangs off (`NODE` or `SYS` edges vs `PIX`/`PXB`). Local DRAM latency is around 80-100 ns; a remote access adds the UPI or Infinity Fabric hop, commonly another 50-100 ns, and remote bandwidth is capped by the interconnect (tens to low hundreds of GB/s) instead of the local channels. Linux allocates pages on the node of the thread that first touches them by default (first-touch), so a buffer allocated by a main thread on node 0 and used by workers on node 1 is remote for all of them. `numactl --cpunodebind=N --membind=N` and `mbind`/`set_mempolicy` fix placement; `MPOL_INTERLEAVE` spreads pages across nodes for bandwidth when the access pattern is uniform. Recent parts subdivide a socket further (sub-NUMA clustering, AMD NPS modes, per-CCD L3), so a socket may report 2-4 nodes; the kernel scheduler prefers to keep a task and its memory together but migrations can undo this. For GPU serving, the standard practice is one host process per GPU pinned to the GPU’s socket, with pinned staging buffers allocated on that node, and the NIC used for RDMA on the same socket so that GPUDirect traffic never traverses the inter-socket link. In VMs, the hypervisor must expose a matching virtual NUMA topology or the guest scheduler makes wrong decisions. `numastat -p <pid>` shows how a process’s pages are split across nodes.',
