    summary: 'DRAM is where the process heap, the page cache holding model files, pinned staging buffers and any CPU-offloaded weights or KV blocks live. A current server socket with 8-12 DDR5 channels delivers on the order of 300-500 GB/s (example: 8 × DDR5-4800 ≈ 307 GB/s; 12 × DDR5-4800 ≈ 460 GB/s) at about 80-100 ns random-access latency. That is roughly 10× below HBM3 on a data-center GPU, which is why anything offloaded to the host runs an order of magnitude slower than on-device.',
    purpose: 'Provide large, cheap capacity (hundreds of GB to a few TB per socket) behind the cache hierarchy and as the staging area for everything that flows to the GPU.',
    mechanics: 'Each memory channel is a 64-bit (DDR5: two 32-bit subchannels) bus to a set of DIMMs; DDR5-4800 gives 4800 MT/s × 8 bytes ≈ 38.4 GB/s per channel, and bandwidth scales with the number of populated channels, not with capacity. Inside a DIMM, banks hold rows of a few KB; an access to an open row is fast and a row miss adds precharge and activate delays, so sequential streams that exploit open rows approach peak while random 64-byte accesses achieve a fraction of it. Latency to a random line is ~80-100 ns local; a core can keep only a limited number of misses in flight (tens of line-fill buffers), so per-core bandwidth is bounded by `outstanding misses × 64 B ÷ latency`, and it takes many cores or hardware prefetch to saturate the socket. `STREAM` or `mlc` measure the practical ceiling, commonly 80-90 percent of nominal. For LLM work the numbers matter in three places: loading weights from the page cache (a 140 GB checkpoint at ~30 GB/s of file-to-pinned-buffer copying is several seconds even before PCIe), CPU or hybrid inference where each decode token streams the offloaded weights through DRAM once (bandwidth ÷ bytes per token bounds tokens/s), and KV offload where evicted blocks are written to and read back from host memory over PCIe at the lower of PCIe and DRAM rates. ECC DIMMs correct single-bit errors and log them (`edac-util`, `dmesg`); correctable-error storms often precede a DIMM failure. Capacity is planned for weights + page cache + pinned buffers + per-request host state, and swapping is fatal for a serving process.',
