    summary: 'The root complex is the part of the CPU that terminates PCIe links and translates between device transactions and memory or core requests. GPUs, NICs and NVMe drives hang off its root ports, sometimes behind PCIe switches, and every host-to-device copy, every doorbell write and every peer-to-peer transfer that does not use NVLink passes through it. Its nominal per-device bandwidth (PCIe 4.0 x16 ≈ 32 GB/s, PCIe 5.0 x16 ≈ 64 GB/s per direction) is 50-100× below HBM, which is why data must be resident on the GPU before compute starts.',
    purpose: 'Connect I/O devices to memory and cores with a coherent, DMA-capable fabric; decide which device pairs can talk directly and at what bandwidth.',
    mechanics: 'A server socket provides a fixed number of lanes (for example 80-128 per socket on current parts) grouped into root ports; an x16 link at PCIe 4.0 (16 GT/s per lane, 128b/130b encoding) carries about 31.5 GB/s per direction nominal, PCIe 5.0 about 63 GB/s, and PCIe 6.0 doubles again with PAM4 signalling. Practical `cudaMemcpy` throughput is lower (commonly 22-26 GB/s on 4.0 x16, 45-55 GB/s on 5.0 x16, example ranges) because of transaction-layer packet overhead, small maximum payload sizes and DMA setup. The CPU accesses device memory through BARs mapped into the physical address space (doorbells, small MMIO writes); bulk data moves the other way, as device-initiated DMA to host DRAM, which requires pinned pages and passes through the IOMMU when it is enabled. Peer-to-peer traffic between two GPUs on the same switch stays inside the switch (`PIX` in `nvidia-smi topo -m`); across root ports of the same socket it is forwarded by the root complex (`PHB`/`NODE`), and across sockets it traverses the CPU interconnect (`SYS`), each step lowering bandwidth and raising latency. Access Control Services (ACS) on switches or root ports can force all P2P traffic up through the root complex and IOMMU for isolation, which disables direct GPU-to-GPU DMA unless the driver’s P2P path is allowed; this is a common cause of slow NCCL on PCIe-only boxes. A x16 device that trains down to x8 or to a lower generation (a bent riser, a firmware setting, power management) silently halves H2D bandwidth; `nvidia-smi -q | grep -A2 "PCIe Generation"` and `lspci -vv` report the negotiated `LnkSta`. GPUDirect RDMA lets a NIC DMA straight into GPU memory through the same fabric, so the NIC and GPU are placed under the same switch or root port for best results. With NVLink present, only host traffic and cross-node egress use PCIe, and the root complex stops being the intra-node bottleneck.',
    input: 'MMIO reads/writes from cores, DMA requests from devices, and peer-to-peer transaction-layer packets.',
    output: 'Routed transactions to DRAM, to cores, or to another device, at link bandwidth minus protocol overhead.',
    tensorShape: 'Per socket: N root ports × up to 16 lanes each; a device link described by generation × width (for example `Gen5 x16`), each lane one serial pair per direction.',
