    name: 'Frequency, turbo & power',
    subtitle: 'P-states, C-states, turbo and thermal limits set how fast a core runs right now; the governor decides policy.',
    subsystem: 'cpu',
    modes: ['system', 'scheduling'],
    aliases: ['DVFS', 'turbo boost', 'C-states', 'P-states', 'cpufreq governor', 'TDP', 'power management'],
    keywords: ['intel_pstate', 'amd-pstate', 'performance governor', 'schedutil', 'turbostat', 'cpupower', 'idle=poll', 'RAPL', 'AVX offset', 'thermal throttling', 'C6'],
    summary: 'A core’s clock is not fixed: it rises under turbo when few cores are active and power allows, falls under heavy vector load or thermal limits, and drops to near zero in deep idle states from which waking takes tens of microseconds. For a serving host the policy that matters is the cpufreq governor (performance vs powersave/schedutil) and the idle-state depth, because a core that has gone to sleep between decode steps adds wake-up latency to the next kernel launch.',
    purpose: 'Trade power and heat for speed dynamically, within a package power budget (TDP) shared by all cores.',
    mechanics: 'P-states are frequency/voltage operating points; the hardware or the kernel (`intel_pstate`, `amd-pstate`, `cpufreq`) selects one per core according to the governor and utilization, with `performance` holding the maximum sustainable frequency and `powersave`/`schedutil` scaling with load. Turbo raises the clock above the base frequency for as long as the package stays within its power (RAPL limits, PL1/PL2 on Intel) and thermal budgets; with all cores busy the all-core turbo is lower than the single-core turbo, and sustained AVX-512 or AMX use applies a further offset on some generations. C-states are idle states: C1 halts the core with fast wake, deeper states (C6 and beyond) flush caches and power-gate, saving power but costing tens to hundreds of microseconds to resume; the kernel’s idle governor picks a depth based on predicted idle time. A serving loop that waits on the GPU for a few milliseconds per step lets its cores drop into deep C-states, so the next `cudaLaunchKernel` or event poll begins with a wake-up penalty and a low starting frequency; `cpupower idle-set -D <n>`, `idle=poll` or busy-wait synchronization (`cudaDeviceScheduleSpin`) trade power for latency. TDP is the sustained package power (for example 250-400 W per server socket); exceeding it throttles frequency, and inadequate cooling produces thermal throttling visible as `turbostat` frequency below nominal with high package temperature. In VMs the guest usually cannot see or control these states; the host’s policy applies. `turbostat`, `cpupower frequency-info` and `/sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu*/cpufreq/` report the current state.',
    input: 'Per-core utilization, package power and temperature, governor policy and idle predictions.',
